  "content": {
    "id": "p17-p2-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "By defining $$h(x)=f(g(x))$$ and using a similar technique, prove the following statements.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-p2-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "The composition of two even functions is even.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$h(-x)=f(g(-x))=f(g(x))=h(x)$, so $h$ is even.",
          "worked": "Since $g$ is even, $g(-x)=g(x)$. Thus $$\\begin{align*}h(-x)&=f(g(-x))\\\\h(-x)&=f(g(x))\\\\h(-x)&=h(x).\\end{align*}$$ Therefore $h$ is even.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 21
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-p2-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "The composition of two odd functions is odd.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$h(-x)=f(-g(x))=-f(g(x))=-h(x)$, so $h$ is odd.",
          "worked": "Both $f$ and $g$ are odd. Hence $$\\begin{align*}h(-x)&=f(g(-x))\\\\h(-x)&=f(-g(x))\\\\h(-x)&=-f(g(x))\\\\h(-x)&=-h(x).\\end{align*}$$ Therefore $h$ is odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-p2-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
